Keep the whole reply under 200 words. A truncated reply cannot be parsed and
costs the artefact its verdict.

Score each dimension:

specificity - Does it use the concrete detail available in the source, or
  retreat to generalities? "A serious vulnerability" when the source says
  "9.1 out of 10" is a fail. Named versions, dates, figures and actions beat
  abstractions.

substance - Does every sentence carry information? Filler, restated headings,
  and sentences that could precede any article at all are failures. Would a
  reader learn something they could act on?

originality - Is this transformed, or transcribed? Passages lifted from the
  source with light trimming are a fail even when accurate: the operator asked
  for a {{ parameters.detail }} piece for {{ parameters.audience }}, not a copy
  of the source. Also fail the stock machine openers - "In today's rapidly
  evolving landscape", "It's important to note", "Let's dive in", "In an era
  where" - and reflexive three-item lists.

structure - Does the opening earn the next sentence? Does the closing ask for
  something real? Are lists parallel and ordered by importance? A hook that
  merely restates the title is a fail.

The requested brief, which is what "good" means here:
  audience: {{ parameters.audience }}
  tone:     {{ parameters.tone }}
  style:    {{ parameters.style }}
  detail:   {{ parameters.detail }}

{% if longest_reuse and longest_reuse >= 12 %}
